  "paper": "paper_2",
  "question": {
    "html": "<section class=\"context\"><p>A satellite moves around Earth in a circular orbit.</p>\n<p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure></p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"a\"><p><strong>a</strong> Draw an arrow on the diagram to represent the direction of the acceleration of the satellite. [1]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b\"><p><strong>b</strong> </p><p>The following data are given:</p>\n<p>Mass of Earth, M = 5.97 × 10<sup>24</sup> kg<br/>                              Radius of Earth, R = 6.37 × 10<sup>6</sup> m<br/>           Orbital period of the satellite, T = 5.62 × 10<sup>3</sup> s</p><p></p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b.i\"><p><strong>b.i</strong> </p><p>Kepler’s Third Law of orbital motion states that <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"T^{2}=kr^{3}\"></span> where <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"k\"></span> is a constant and <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"r\"></span> is the orbital radius of the satellite.</p>\n<p>Show that <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"k=\\frac{4\\pi^{2}}{GM}\"></span>.</p><p>[1]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b.ii\"><p><strong>b.ii</strong> Determine the height of the satellite above the Earth’s surface. [2]</p></section>"
